We designed two completely separate power-capture pathways for these ties:

1.  **Mechanical Acoustic Harvesting (What the 3D model is showing):** The physical wheel impact snapping the bistable buckling beams and triggering the ZnO nanowires. As we calculated earlier, this yields about **$31.2\text{ Joules}$** per axle pass. Spread over the duration of the impact, this translates to peak kinetic power bursts of around **$30\text{ to }50\text{ Watts}$** per tie. This is a low-power "trickle charge" meant to keep the sensors and telemetry alive autonomously.
2.  **Inductive Regenerative Braking (Not currently animated):** This is the massive **$12\text{ to }16\text{ Megawatt}$** electromagnetic energy dump from a slowing locomotive. Spread across 1,000 ties, *this* is where the $16.2\text{ kW}$ per tie figure comes from.

Since the 3D visualizer is currently demonstrating the mechanical axle impact and the buckling beam snap-through, displaying $16,200\text{ W}$ is completely disproportionate to the physics happening on screen.

Let's fix the telemetry so it accurately reflects the $30 - 50\text{ W}$ piezoelectric trickle-charge bursts.

